# Target C — aggregate safe-lattice repacking across all inactive chambers

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Status:** proof-only research theorem; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.

The previous Type-I repacking route asked one macroscopic inactive chamber to absorb a
vertex cover of the complete actual diameter graph. That is unnecessarily local.
Canonical Section 77 already partitions every contact-isolated source point inside the
force hull among pairwise interior-disjoint inactive chambers. The contact-wall and
hull-outer-pocket mixed-wall theorems give a safe replacement region in every such
chamber.

Use **one translated triangular lattice in the union of all safe regions**. Then
cross-chamber new-new separation is automatic, and translation averaging realizes the
total safe area without a separate integer floor loss in every chamber.

The result is an exact global criterion. It also converts the chamber-residual
conservation law into a necessary source-level inequality for every survivor.

This theorem concerns the Section-77 chambers inside the force hull: bounded
nonquadrilateral contact faces and Section-67 hull-augmentation outer pockets. It does
not include the distinct exterior caps in `K\setminus int F` from Sections 77.2/80/162.

---

## 1. Canonical chamber data

Use canonical Sections 67 and 77. Let

- `F` be the force hull and `K=conv(A)` the full source hull;
- `Z_ch=N_F-m` be the full contact-isolated population assigned to inactive chambers
  inside `F`;
- `f` be the number of bounded nonquadrilateral faces;
- `Q=sum(deg C-4)` over those faces;
- `a` be the number of hull sides added in the Section-67 augmentation, equivalently
  the number of hull-augmentation outer pockets;
- `b` be the occurrence-counted old outer contact-walk length;
- `q=|B|` be the loaded force-hull vertex count;
- `k=d_H+s_Z` be the canonical topology variable;
- `L_add` be the total Euclidean length of the added force-hull sides.

For each internal exceptional face `C_j`, let `m_j=deg C_j`. For each outer pocket
`P_j`, write its boundary as one added side of length `s_j` plus a complementary old
unit-contact path with `ell_j` edges.

Canonical incidence identities give

\[
 \sum_{exc}m_j=4f+Q,
\tag{1.1}
\]

\[
 \sum_{out}(\ell_j+1)=b+2a-q,
\qquad
 \sum_{out}\ell_j=b+a-q,
\tag{1.2}
\]

\[
 \sum_{out}s_j=L_{add}.
\tag{1.3}
\]

Define the total unit-contact boundary length counted in the chamber safe-area theorem
by

\[
 L_c:=(4f+Q)+(b+a-q).
\tag{1.4}
\]

Using canonical `b+Q=2(q-k)`, this also has the exact form

\[
 \boxed{L_c=4f+q-2k+a.}
\tag{1.5}
\]

---

## 2. Safe regions and one common lattice

For an internal exceptional face, let

\[
 S_j=\{y\in\operatorname{int}C_j:|y-p|\ge1
 \text{ for every boundary source vertex }p\}.
\tag{2.1}
\]

For a hull-augmentation outer pocket with added side `I_j` and old contact-path
vertices `p`, let

\[
 S_j=\{y\in\operatorname{int}C_j:
 \operatorname{dist}(y,I_j)\ge1,
 \ |y-p|\ge1\text{ for every old path vertex }p\}.
\tag{2.2}
\]

The companion wall-shield theorems prove that every point in `S_j` is unit-separated
from every old source point outside `int C_j`.

The inactive chamber interiors are pairwise disjoint. Put

\[
 S:=\bigcup_jS_j.
\tag{2.3}
\]

Let `Lambda` be the unit triangular lattice, of covolume

\[
 a_0={\sqrt3\over2}.
\]

For every translate `Lambda+t`, all points of `(Lambda+t) cap S` are pairwise
unit-separated **globally**, including points in different chambers. Translation
averaging gives

\[
 \boxed{
 \max_t |(\Lambda+t)\cap S|
 \ge\left\lfloor{\operatorname{area}(S)\over a_0}\right\rfloor.
 }
\tag{2.4}
\]

This is stronger than averaging each chamber separately: there is only one floor.

---

## 3. Aggregate diameter-cover repacking

Let `H` be the complete actual diameter graph and let `U` be a minimum vertex cover,
so `|U|=tau(H)`.

Canonical Section 77 has `Z_ch=A cap F\setminus V(G)` after chamber assignment, while
every actual diameter endpoint is loaded and lies in the positive core. Hence

\[
 Z_{ch}\cap U=\varnothing.
\tag{3.1}
\]

### Theorem 3.1 — aggregate inactive-chamber repacking

If

\[
 \boxed{
 \left\lfloor{\operatorname{area}(S)\over a_0}\right\rfloor
 \ge Z_{ch}+\tau(H),
 }
\tag{3.2}
\]

then the source is not a global diameter minimizer.

#### Proof

Choose one lattice translate whose intersection with `S` contains at least the
right-hand side of (3.2), and retain exactly `Z_ch+tau(H)` of those lattice points;
call the resulting set `Y`. Define

\[
 A':=(A\setminus(Z_{ch}\cup U))\cup Y.
\tag{3.3}
\]

Equation (3.1) gives `|A'|=|A|`.

The new points are pairwise unit-separated because they lie in one translate of the
unit triangular lattice. Let `y in Y` lie in chamber `C_j`. Every retained old source
point lies outside `int C_j`: every old source point assigned to the chamber has been
removed, and positive-core points lie on or outside its boundary. The appropriate
wall-shield theorem therefore gives distance at least one from `y` to every retained
old source point. Thus `A'` is unit-separated.

The cover `U` meets every actual diameter edge, so every retained old-old distance is
strictly below `D`. Every new point lies in the interior of one chamber inside `F`,
hence in `int F subset int K`. An interior point of `K` is at distance strictly below
`D` from every point of `K`. Thus every pair involving a new point is also strictly
below `D`.

The finite set `A'` therefore has diameter strictly below `D`, contradicting the
definition of `D(n)`. `square`

The competitor is not required to remain triangle-free.

---

## 4. Exact total safe-area lower bound

Put

\[
 c_*={\pi\over6}+{\sqrt3\over4},
 \qquad
 h_*={\pi\over3}-{\sqrt3\over4}.
\tag{4.1}
\]

The contact-chain area theorem gives for each internal face

\[
 area(S_j)\ge A_j-m_jc_*-h_*,
\tag{4.2}
\]

and for each hull-augmentation outer pocket

\[
 area(S_j)\ge A_j-s_j-\ell_jc_*-h_*.
\tag{4.3}
\]

Because chamber interiors are disjoint and their total area is `A_ch`, summing
(4.2)--(4.3), then using (1.1)--(1.4), gives

\[
 \boxed{
 area(S)
 \ge A_{ch}-c_*L_c-L_{add}-h_*(f+a).
 }
\tag{4.4}
\]

This bound is finite and uses no Type-I asymptotics.

---

## 5. Residual-conservation form

For each chamber let

\[
 r_j=\sigma_j-k_j\ge0
\]

be its augmented-boundary Oler residual. The companion conservation theorem proves

\[
 \boxed{
 \sum_jr_j=R_F,
 \qquad
 R_F={A_F\over a_0}+{P_F\over2}+1-N_F-{E_4\over a_0}\ge0.
 }
\tag{5.1}
\]

Define the exact constants

\[
 \kappa_*={\pi\over3\sqrt3},
 \qquad
 \lambda_*={2\over\sqrt3}+{1\over2},
\tag{5.2}
\]

\[
 C_*={1\over2}+{2\pi\over3\sqrt3},
 \qquad
 \eta_*={2\pi\over3\sqrt3}-{1\over2}={h_*\over a_0}.
\tag{5.3}
\]

For an internal face,

\[
 {area(S_j)\over a_0}-z_j
 \ge r_j-\kappa_*m_j-C_*.
\tag{5.4}
\]

For an outer pocket,

\[
 {area(S_j)\over a_0}-z_j
 \ge r_j-\kappa_*\ell_j-\lambda_*s_j-\eta_*.
\tag{5.5}
\]

Summing and using (1.1)--(1.3) and (5.1),

\[
 \boxed{
 {area(S)\over a_0}-Z_{ch}
 \ge \Xi,
 }
\tag{5.6}
\]

where

\[
 \boxed{
 \Xi:=R_F
 -\kappa_*L_c
 -\lambda_*L_{add}
 -C_*f
 -\eta_*a.
 }
\tag{5.7}
\]

Equivalently, by (1.5),

\[
 \boxed{
 \Xi=R_F
 -\kappa_*(4f+q-2k+a)
 -\lambda_*L_{add}
 -C_*f
 -\eta_*a.
 }
\tag{5.8}
\]

Because `Z_ch` is an integer,

\[
 \left\lfloor{area(S)\over a_0}\right\rfloor-Z_{ch}
 =\left\lfloor{area(S)\over a_0}-Z_{ch}\right\rfloor
 \ge\lfloor\Xi\rfloor.
\tag{5.9}
\]

Combining with Theorem 3.1 gives the finite closing criterion

\[
 \boxed{
 \Xi\ge\tau(H)
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 \operatorname{diameter\ minimality\ contradiction}.
 }
\tag{5.10}
\]

Hence every surviving global minimizer necessarily satisfies

\[
 \boxed{
 \Xi<\tau(H)
 \le\left\lceil{q\over2}\right\rceil.
 }
\tag{5.11}
\]

This is the useful source-level form: all Section-77 inactive chambers share one
residual, one lattice translate, and one diameter-cover requirement.

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The Type-I inactive route no longer needs a theorem of the form

> one macroscopic chamber has at least `tau(H)` spare slots.

For the internal force-hull chamber system it is enough to prove the single scalar
inequality `Xi>=tau(H)`. Conversely, any survivor must obey the strict upper bound
(5.11).

What remains is to control `R_F` relative to the exact boundary costs in `Xi`. The
canonical statement `R_F=O(D)` alone is not enough because every term in (5.7) is also
boundary order.

The distinct Section-80/162 exterior-cap alternative remains outside this theorem and
continues to use the full-collar Section-165 repacking criterion.

No contact-isolated source point is placed under the Section-57 affine-kernel theorem,
and no source point is lost in the competitor.
